## Testing standard

- A feature is done when tests prove it works the way a user uses it. Green service-level tests alone are not enough: they have hidden buttons that end in a 403, guards bypassed through another page and steps a user could skip.
- For every feature cover the happy path; every role that may act and every role that may not; validation and failure modes; edge cases (empty states, boundaries, duplicates); and side effects (persisted state, audit or log entries, notifications, queued jobs).
- Every user-facing flow also gets a browser test that drives the real UI: sign in, navigate, click, fill in, assert what the user sees.
- Name tests by behaviour in domain language, one behaviour per test. When a test exposes a bug or an unfinished feature, say so explicitly.
- Under `RefreshDatabase`, Laravel's testing transaction manager treats transaction level 1 as the outermost: after-commit work (`DB::afterCommit()`, jobs, listeners and notifications marked `afterCommit`) runs as soon as a nested transaction commits back to level 1, even while an application transaction on that level is still open. A test can't show that such work waits for an outer transaction. Code that wraps a long run in one transaction (a seeder, an import) must not expect queued after-commit jobs to have run before it ends: run that work inline, or outside the transaction.
@if($assist->hasPackage('pestphp/pest'))
- Pest invokes closures in a dataset before passing them, except to a parameter typed `Closure`, which receives the closure itself. Don't wrap it in a second closure.
- Pest loads every test file into one process. Two files declaring a global helper function of the same name each pass alone and break the whole suite with a fatal redeclaration error. Give file-local helpers distinctive names and keep shared ones in `tests/Pest.php`; `grep -rhoE "^function \w+" tests | sort | uniq -d` lists clashes.
- `expect($object)->value` is the expectation's own `value` property (the object itself), not the object's `value`. Write `expect($object->value)`.
- `php artisan test --parallel --shard=k/n` fails: artisan passes its `--runner` option on to the `--list-tests` call Pest's sharding makes, and PHPUnit rejects it. Shard with `vendor/bin/pest --parallel --processes=N --shard=k/n`, which uses Laravel's parallel runner just the same. Pest splits shards by test file, not by run time: when one shard runs far longer than the others, record timings with `--update-shards`.
- Pest runs no test from a `<file>` entry of a phpunit.xml test suite: `--testsuite` lists and runs none of them, without a warning (seen with Pest 5.2 and PHPUnit 13). Build test suites, such as named groups for CI jobs, from `<directory>` entries, and guard the grouping with a test that holds every test file to exactly one suite and forbids single files, or tests drop out of CI unnoticed.
@endif
@if($assist->hasPackage('livewire/livewire', '^4.0'))

@scoped(['tests/**'])
## Livewire tests

- A component under `Livewire::test()` runs `assertSee()`, `assertDontSee()`, `assertSeeText()`, `assertDontSeeText()`, `assertSeeHtml()`, `assertDontSeeHtml()` and `assertSeeHtmlInOrder()` against its current HTML. Every other response assertion, `assertSeeInOrder()` and `assertSeeTextInOrder()` among them, goes to the last HTTP response: it passes on the first render and fails after `set()` or `call()`, whose response is the update's JSON (escaped markup such as `<\/p>` and `\u00fc`, plus the snapshot). Check order with `assertSeeHtmlInOrder()` (texts through `e()`) or in `$component->html()`.
- `Livewire::withQueryParams()`, `withCookies()` and `withHeaders()` stay set for every later `Livewire::test()` in the same test. Call `withQueryParams()` before each `test()`, with an empty array where none apply, or a later component starts with the earlier ones.
- A page whose record doesn't exist, or that a resource query hides, answers `Livewire::test()` with a 404 response, not an exception: Laravel's handler turns the `ModelNotFoundException` into one. Assert `assertNotFound()`; `expect(fn () => …)->toThrow()` never sees an exception.
@endscoped
@endif
@if($assist->hasPackage('filament/filament', '^5.0'))

@scoped(['tests/**'])
## Filament tests

- Filament silently ignores a request to mount or call an action the user can't see (hidden, unauthorized or not found): no 403, no exception. A test that calls a forbidden action and checks that nothing changed also passes when the action's name is wrong, so also assert the action hidden for that user and visible for one who may use it. The test helpers can't resolve a hidden action attached to a schema component (a section header, an entry) at all; assert that its label is missing from the page.
- A field's test key is its absolute key: a parent layout component's `key()` becomes part of it unless set with `isInheritable: false`, and field assertions such as `assertFormFieldExists()` then miss the field.
- `goToNextWizardStep()` sets the wizard's next step before validating the current one and leaves it set when `afterValidation()` throws `Halt`: `assertWizardCurrentStep()` then reports the next step although the browser stays, since the browser moves only on the `next-wizard-step` event. After a halted step assert `assertNotDispatched('next-wizard-step')`, and `assertDispatched('next-wizard-step')` where the step does move, so the negative check can fail.
- An action whose submission fails validation stays mounted on the test component. A second `callAction()` for it on the same component then looks for the action inside the mounted one and fails with "Action [name] was not found for action [name]". Test the refused and the successful submission on separate `Livewire::test()` instances.
@endscoped
@endif
@if($assist->hasPackage('pestphp/pest-plugin-browser'))

@scoped(['tests/**'])
## Browser tests (pest-plugin-browser)

- The in-process server shares the test's application, so the session store, auth guards, tenancy and Filament's scoped services carry over from one request to the next unless the base test case resets them per request: the navigation (`NavigationManager`) and the request it marks the current page by (`originalRequest`; kept, the menu marks the first page of the session as the current one on every page). `actingAs()` then signs in every browser context: don't mix it with signing in through the login form. Base test case and helpers: `filament-browser-testing` skill.
- A click doesn't wait for a Livewire redirect. Assert something the next page shows before the next step.
- Playwright acts as soon as an element exists, and `assertSee()` and `assertPathIs()` pass while the next page is still loading. A field filled before Livewire starts is emptied when it starts (it writes the form's empty state into its fields), and a button clicked then does nothing. pest-plugin-browser also ends `navigate()` on any page's `load` event, including the late one of the page being left, so the next step can run on a page that hasn't loaded. Keep every page hidden until its `load` event in the base test case; assertions, fills and clicks then wait for the loaded page (`filament-browser-testing` skill). Without it, suites pass on fast machines and fail at random on CI.
- `script()` does not throw when a navigation replaces the page while it runs: it returns the browser's error text, a string, in place of the value. To read from a page just opened with `navigate()`, mark the page being left first (a property on `window`), then read until a wholly loaded page without the mark answers (`filament-browser-testing` skill, `readAfterNavigation()`).
- `assertSee()` matches any visible text containing the string, case ignored (Playwright's `getByText`): `assertSee('Confirmed')` already passes on a prompt reading "… must be confirmed.". To wait for a state, assert the exact element: `assertVisible('text="Confirmed"')`.
- `assertSee()` does not find the text of the option a native `<select>` shows as chosen. Check the choice with `assertValue()` on the select.
- Never travel back in time in a browser test: the browser drops session cookies that expired in the travelled past.
- Reach tenants as `http://<subdomain>.localhost:<port>` (Chromium resolves every `*.localhost` to loopback) and set the tenant base domain to `localhost` before service providers boot.
- Select Filament fields by id: `[id="form.email"]`, `[id="mountedActionSchema0.name"]`. A dotted name is otherwise parsed as CSS. Searchable selects are a button plus an option list, not a native select.
- A Builder or Repeater is a field itself, wrapping its items' fields: a selector by label such as `.fi-fo-field:has(label:has-text("…"))` also matches the builder around the field, and so every field nested in it. Select nested fields by id (`[id*=".conditions."][id$=".field"]`). `nth=` waits only for a position that doesn't exist yet: a block inserted before others, or into a nested builder, is found at once at the old block's position, so select it by an id pattern too (`filament-browser-testing` skill).
- `click()`, `fill()` and the other element methods take a string that starts with `#`, `.` or `[`, or contains one of `: ( ) , = > + ~ * | ^`, or a dot before a letter ("z.B."), as a CSS selector, and anything else as visible text. Click such a text through Playwright's text engine, quoted for an exact match: `text="Temperature: 9.5 °C"`. Unquoted, it also matches every longer text containing it, and strict mode fails on two matches. `assertSee()` always searches text. A bare tag name is text too: `keys('body', …)` waits for an element showing "body"; press page-wide keys on an element found by class or id (`.fi-body` in a Filament panel).
- Playwright dismisses the browser's own dialogs, so a button with a confirm dialog (Livewire's `wire:confirm`) silently does nothing. Set `window.confirm` to answer true before the click (`filament-browser-testing` skill).
- The in-process server hands the app a multipart request's raw body but none of its files (an open TODO in pest-plugin-browser), so every Livewire or Filament upload arrives empty and fails. Parse multipart bodies in the base test case into `Illuminate\Http\UploadedFile` objects created with `test: true` (a Symfony `UploadedFile` is rebuilt without its test flag, and validation then rejects a file PHP didn't upload), and create Livewire's test upload disk in `setUp()` with `FileUploadConfiguration::storage()`. Code and upload helpers: `filament-browser-testing` skill.
- Filament renders on the server for every round trip; raise the browser timeout above the 5-second default.
- A Filament button with `labeled-from` renders twice, icon-only and labelled, with one of them hidden at each screen width. A selector by its `href` or label matches both, and strict mode fails: add `:visible`.
- Run WebAuthn/passkey flows with a software authenticator so the server verifies real signatures; never stub the verification.
- Playwright refuses to click an element that another element covers ("… intercepts pointer events") and retries until the timeout, even when both are links to the same address. Where links are stretched over a card, stretch only one per card, and click that one.
- A Livewire lazy component (Filament's widgets are lazy by default) and a Filament upload field (FilePond, loaded when it becomes visible) appear only once scrolled into view. A check that measures or screenshots a whole page scrolls through it and waits for them first, or it measures their placeholders.
- Filament scrolls to the first validation error 200 ms after it appears; up to 5.10.1 the handler scrolls the error node's field wrapper without checking that the node is still on the page (5.10.2 checks). A browser test that corrects a refused form and resubmits within that window, so that the response without the error has morphed the node away, gets `Uncaught TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'scrollIntoView')`, once per schema handling the event, and `assertNoJavaScriptErrors()` fails or passes with the timing. Below 5.10.2, pause a moment after asserting the error before correcting the form, as a person reads it first.
@endscoped
